  "worked_solution": "We're given that $x=2$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-32x+12,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+48x-32 \\\\  & =4({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-8) \\\\  & =4(x-2)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(x)=12(x-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(2) & =12(2-2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is zero, we conclude that the test is inconclusive, we need to use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's  find the stationary points. These points are the solution of the equation ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-2)^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the only stationary point is $x=2.$\n\nLet's choose auxiliary points from each side of the stationary point and evaluate the first derivative at them. We take the auxiliary points $x=1,3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(1) & =4(1-2)^{3}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(3) & =4(3-2)^{3}=4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the first derivative changes the sign at $2$  from minus to plus, we conclude that $x=2$  is a relative minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
